 ## The base

-Start with tax-adjusted taxable income, not turnover. Section 28(1), replaced in 2023, permits noncapital expenditure/losses wholly, exclusively and necessarily incurred for trade or income production. Section 28(2) permits deduction only for expenditure producing taxable income when taxable and exempt income coexist, and disallows expenditure related to income subject to a final tax. If common expenditure cannot be directly attributed, the deductible share is common expenditure multiplied by taxable income divided by total income. If that formula gives an unreasonable result, the Commissioner General may approve or direct another apportionment method. Apply those restrictions before using the adjusted taxable-income input. Act 3/2026 s 4 also inserts section 45(l) into the deductions-not-allowed provision: add back any levy payable under the Taxation Act or any other written law. Reconcile this with the levies actually charged in the accounts before fixing the taxable-income input. [Taxation 45](https://elibrary.rat.gov.mw/server/api/core/bitstreams/06b35bf5-faa2-4fa1-81f7-af650cce4dff/content); [Act 3/2026 s 4](https://media.malawilii.org/media/legislation/15519/source_file/2e96ab6e73cdf961/mw-act-2026-3-publication-document.pdf). Reconcile accounting depreciation, capital allowances, exemptions, capital gains and non-allowable costs separately. General capital gains enter section 11 income; the old listed-share holding-period exemption in First Schedule(hb) was removed by Act 36/2025 s 5, so do not assume twelve-month holdings exempt. Detailed gain/basis rules remain outside this working paper. [2023 s 3](https://elibrary.rat.gov.mw/server/api/core/bitstreams/72477a6a-7348-4dd8-8a05-a7ea265a5e00/content); [2025 s 5](https://elibrary.rat.gov.mw/server/api/core/bitstreams/f9c5b41b-5dce-4764-b7c2-923c808bda48/content).
+Start with tax-adjusted taxable income, not turnover. Section 28(1), replaced in 2023, permits noncapital expenditure/losses wholly, exclusively and necessarily incurred for trade or income production. Section 28(2) permits deduction only for expenditure producing taxable income when taxable and exempt income coexist, and disallows expenditure related to income subject to a final tax. If common expenditure cannot be directly attributed, the deductible share is common expenditure multiplied by taxable income divided by total income. If that formula gives an unreasonable result, the Commissioner General may approve or direct another apportionment method. Apply those restrictions before using the adjusted taxable-income input. Act 3/2026 s 4 also inserts section 45(l) into the deductions-not-allowed provision: add back any levy payable under the Taxation Act or any other written law. Reconcile this with the levies actually charged in the accounts before fixing the taxable-income input. [Taxation 45](https://elibrary.rat.gov.mw/server/api/core/bitstreams/06b35bf5-faa2-4fa1-81f7-af650cce4dff/content); [Act 3/2026 s 4](https://media.malawilii.org/media/legislation/15519/source_file/2e96ab6e73cdf961/mw-act-2026-3-publication-document.pdf). Reconcile accounting depreciation, capital allowances, exemptions, capital gains and non-allowable costs separately. General capital gains enter section 11 income; the listed-share holding-period exemption position remains unresolved. Act 36/2025 s 5 literally deletes First Schedule (hb), while the historical principal labels the listed-share holding-period exemption (hc). The intervening paragraph-label chain is not established, so this draft supports neither removal nor continuing availability of that exemption and does not approve an earlier or straddling gain calculation. See [listed-share withholding](mw-listed-share-withholding.md), including its currentness hold. Detailed gain/basis rules remain outside this working paper. [2023 s 3](https://elibrary.rat.gov.mw/server/api/core/bitstreams/72477a6a-7348-4dd8-8a05-a7ea265a5e00/content); [Act 36/2025 s 5, printed p 4](https://elibrary.rat.gov.mw/server/api/core/bitstreams/f9c5b41b-5dce-4764-b7c2-923c808bda48/content); [historical principal First Schedule (hb)–(hd), printed p 86](https://elibrary.rat.gov.mw/server/api/core/bitstreams/06b35bf5-faa2-4fa1-81f7-af650cce4dff/content).

 Compute ordinary tax, adding the marginal excess charge. For a company meeting both MAT conditions, turnover includes exempt amounts but excludes final-withholding payments (66A(2)). Compute MAT. Apply only valid, unexpired prior MAT credit to ordinary income tax, capped at ordinary tax less current MAT, then pay the higher result and MAT. If current MAT exceeds ordinary income tax, section 66B grants the difference as a new credit. Track origin, use, expiry and FIFO; never refund or use a credit to reduce liability below current MAT. [Sections 66–66B](https://elibrary.rat.gov.mw/server/api/core/bitstreams/f9c5b41b-5dce-4764-b7c2-923c808bda48/content).
